\f0\b Request: 
\f1\b0 I am a process engineer at a chemical plant, and I need to tell the maintenance supervisor when a shell-and-tube heat exchanger should be taken offline for cleaning. Using 30 days of synthetic hourly temperature and flow readings, I will use Visual Studio Code to run a notebook that calculates heat duty, log-mean temperature difference and the overall heat transfer coefficient, checks that the hot-side and cold-side energy balances agree within 5 percent, plots the coefficient over time against a 20 percent fouling limit, and fits a trend line to estimate the day the limit is crossed. I will then record the key results and the recommended cleaning date in a one-page Microsoft Word memo, and build a two-slide PowerPoint briefing with the chart and the recommendation, exported as a PDF.\
\

\f0\b Task Description: 
\f1\b0 \
1. Open the prepared notebook in Visual Studio Code, load the 30-day exchanger CSV, inspect the columns and units, and remove shutdown hours where flow is near zero.\
2. Run the helper functions in Visual Studio Code to calculate hot-side and cold-side duty, log-mean temperature difference and the overall heat transfer coefficient for every hour.\
3. Check in Visual Studio Code that hot-side and cold-side duties agree within 5 percent, and review the readings outside that tolerance.\
4. Set the clean-condition coefficient from the first three days, draw the 20 percent fouling limit, plot the coefficient over time, and fit a trend line to find the day the limit is crossed.\
5. Fill the one-page maintenance memo template in Microsoft Word with a table of key values, the chart, and the recommended cleaning date with a safety margin.\
6. Complete the two-slide briefing in PowerPoint with the chart and finding on slide 1 and the recommendation and risk on slide 2, then export it as a PDF.\
7. Verify the result by hand-calculating one hourly reading in Visual Studio Code and confirming the numbers match across the notebook, memo and slides.\
}
